1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,819
Security in Orchard Core is not one setting.

2
00:00:03,819 --> 00:00:08,103
It is a set of pages in the admin, each covering one part of the site.

3
00:00:08,103 --> 00:00:12,500
This video is a checklist of those pages, with what to look for on each.

4
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,087
The checklist asks four questions of your site:

5
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:23,976
who can do what, how do people sign in, how are connections and

6
00:00:23,976 --> 00:00:27,414
requests protected, and what is kept safe and recorded.

7
00:00:27,414 --> 00:00:31,810
The pages that answer them sit under Access Control, Settings and Tools.

8
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:40,242
Many security features stay off until you enable them.

9
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:46,510
In Tools, Features, the Security group lists them, such as HTTPS, CORS Configuration,

10
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:52,020
ReCaptcha and Rate Limits, each with a button that enables or disables it.

11
00:00:52,476 --> 00:00:55,912
A feature adds its pages to the admin menu only when it is

12
00:00:55,912 --> 00:00:59,456
enabled, so a missing page can mean that its feature is off.

13
00:01:03,639 --> 00:01:05,230
Start with roles.

14
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:10,557
Under Access Control, Roles, each role is a named group of permissions.

15
00:01:10,557 --> 00:01:17,269
Anonymous is every visitor who is not signed in, and Authenticated is every signed-in user.

16
00:01:17,731 --> 00:01:18,937
Open Anonymous.

17
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:24,451
Each permission allowed here is open to every visitor, and signed-in users receive it too.

18
00:01:24,451 --> 00:01:26,921
Keep this list to what visitors need.

19
00:01:27,437 --> 00:01:30,367
Then open Editor and search for Manage Roles.

20
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:35,824
Permissions marked Security Critical could let a user elevate their other permissions, so

21
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:40,017
grant them with extreme consideration, and only to roles you trust.

22
00:01:41,626 --> 00:01:43,728
Next, Access Control, Users.

23
00:01:43,728 --> 00:01:46,448
Each account is listed with its roles.

24
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:51,518
Check that every account still belongs to someone who needs access, and that

25
00:01:51,518 --> 00:01:54,486
its roles match the work that person does.

26
00:01:54,941 --> 00:02:00,459
To remove access, open an account's Actions menu and choose Disable, which blocks

27
00:02:00,459 --> 00:02:02,742
sign-in but keeps the account.

28
00:02:02,742 --> 00:02:07,181
The filters can show only enabled users, or only disabled users.

29
00:02:11,391 --> 00:02:15,101
Settings, Security, User Login sets how people sign in.

30
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:20,879
Disable local password login turns passwords off when everyone uses an external provider,

31
00:02:20,879 --> 00:02:24,711
so keep at least one administrator with a linked account.

32
00:02:25,194 --> 00:02:29,797
With a two-factor method enabled, a Two-Factor Authentication tab appears.

33
00:02:29,797 --> 00:02:35,895
Require two-factor authentication specific roles lets you choose roles, such as Administrator, whose

34
00:02:35,895 --> 00:02:39,404
users are sent to set it up the next time they sign in.

35
00:02:41,046 --> 00:02:45,322
The Users Registration feature lets visitors create their own accounts.

36
00:02:45,322 --> 00:02:50,375
On the Registration page, decide whether Users must verify their email address, which

37
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:55,206
blocks sign-in until the address is confirmed, and whether Users must be approved

38
00:02:55,206 --> 00:02:56,816
before they can log in.

39
00:03:01,027 --> 00:03:07,241
On the HTTPS page, Require HTTPS redirects plain HTTP requests to a secure address.

40
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:11,570
HSTS mode controls the Strict Transport Security header.

41
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:17,365
Browsers that receive it may stop connecting over HTTP if HTTPS later becomes

42
00:03:17,365 --> 00:03:20,297
unavailable, so use it with caution.

43
00:03:20,756 --> 00:03:26,320
These options are disabled, because this page was opened over plain HTTP, and

44
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:31,952
a warning says that changing HTTPS-only settings over HTTP has been prevented.

45
00:03:31,952 --> 00:03:35,036
Open the page over HTTPS to change them.

46
00:03:36,657 --> 00:03:39,932
Security Headers tells browsers how to treat your pages.

47
00:03:39,932 --> 00:03:44,739
Content Security Policy lists what a page may load, Permissions Policy limits browser

48
00:03:44,739 --> 00:03:49,229
features such as the camera, and Referrer Policy controls what a browser shares

49
00:03:49,229 --> 00:03:51,077
when a visitor leaves a page.

50
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:56,638
If a proxy or load balancer sits in front of your site, Reverse

51
00:03:56,638 --> 00:04:00,269
Proxy decides which forwarded headers the site processes:

52
00:04:00,269 --> 00:04:03,843
X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-Host.

53
00:04:03,843 --> 00:04:06,840
Turn on only the headers your own proxy sends.

54
00:04:07,292 --> 00:04:09,387
This matters for rate limits too.

55
00:04:09,387 --> 00:04:14,384
Without the forwarded address, every anonymous visitor appears to come from the address

56
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:17,232
of the proxy, and all of them share one budget.

57
00:04:21,423 --> 00:04:23,734
reCaptcha helps keep robots out.

58
00:04:23,734 --> 00:04:28,295
Enter the Site Key and Secret Key from your Google reCaptcha account.

59
00:04:28,295 --> 00:04:34,436
With the ReCaptcha Users feature enabled, the sign-in, registration and password recovery pages

60
00:04:34,436 --> 00:04:36,443
then ask for the challenge.

61
00:04:38,057 --> 00:04:41,875
Rate Limits caps how many requests a client can send.

62
00:04:41,875 --> 00:04:47,569
Under Tools, Rate Limits, the Default Global Policy limits each signed-in user, or

63
00:04:47,569 --> 00:04:51,257
each IP address for anonymous visitors, per minute.

64
00:04:51,718 --> 00:04:57,952
Read-only Policies lists the limits that features add themselves, such as the ones for signing in.

65
00:04:57,952 --> 00:05:02,328
Add Policy creates your own, for example for a path such as an API.

66
00:05:03,921 --> 00:05:09,415
CORS decides which other websites may call your site from a visitor's browser.

67
00:05:09,415 --> 00:05:14,910
On the Cross-Origin Resource Sharing page, Add a policy, and list each allowed

68
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:18,181
origin instead of choosing Allow any origin.

69
00:05:18,636 --> 00:05:23,623
Allow credentials lets requests carry credentials such as cookies, and the page does

70
00:05:23,623 --> 00:05:26,393
not allow it together with Allow any origin.

71
00:05:26,393 --> 00:05:30,106
Allow only the methods and headers that the other site needs.

72
00:05:34,336 --> 00:05:36,945
Audit Trail logs changes and events.

73
00:05:36,945 --> 00:05:42,039
In Settings, Audit Trail, Events to record chooses what is logged, and Login

74
00:05:42,039 --> 00:05:44,586
failed stays off until you tick it.

75
00:05:45,052 --> 00:05:49,523
Old events are removed on a schedule, and the Trimming tab controls it.

76
00:05:49,523 --> 00:05:53,704
Retention period sets how many days an event is kept, and ten days

77
00:05:53,704 --> 00:05:57,362
is the default, so raise it if you need a longer history.

78
00:05:57,815 --> 00:06:00,641
The log itself is under Tools, Audit Trail.

79
00:06:00,641 --> 00:06:04,737
Each event shows what happened, who did it and when, and the list

80
00:06:04,737 --> 00:06:07,160
can be filtered by date or category.

81
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:09,525
Administrators cannot edit the log.

82
00:06:11,115 --> 00:06:14,161
Keep passwords and API keys as secrets.

83
00:06:14,161 --> 00:06:20,387
Under Tools, Security, Secrets, each value is encrypted and never displayed again, and

84
00:06:20,387 --> 00:06:23,432
settings refer to a secret by its name.

85
00:06:23,432 --> 00:06:27,695
Migrate Secrets moves credentials still kept in settings.

86
00:06:31,895 --> 00:06:35,553
Keep Anonymous short, and disable accounts nobody uses.

87
00:06:35,553 --> 00:06:42,028
Require two-factor authentication for administrators, and decide whether users must verify their email

88
00:06:42,028 --> 00:06:45,265
address and whether registrations need approval.

89
00:06:46,015 --> 00:06:52,927
Require HTTPS, set security headers, and enable only the forwarded headers your proxy sends.

90
00:06:52,927 --> 00:06:57,017
Add reCaptcha, rate limits and a narrow CORS policy.

91
00:06:57,017 --> 00:07:00,755
Record events, and keep credentials as secrets.

92
00:07:02,755 --> 00:07:04,305
That is the checklist:

93
00:07:04,305 --> 00:07:08,457
who can do what, how people sign in, how connections and requests are

94
00:07:08,457 --> 00:07:11,058
protected, and what is kept and recorded.

95
00:07:11,058 --> 00:07:14,656
Review these pages whenever your people or features change.

96
00:07:14,656 --> 00:07:16,095
Thanks for watching.

